WEBVTT
NOTE OpenIE Research TV v3.0 — eco-database (90s) Narrator Osato

1
00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:07.855
First, most software burns needless time and power unpacking text and recalculating answers it already solved.

2
00:00:08.355 --> 00:00:15.930
Second, fingerprint-chained logs and dual-time indexes let any machine replay exact past states without guesswork.

3
00:00:16.430 --> 00:00:24.053
Third, zero-copy memory-mapped lookup stores turn repeated work into sub-microsecond reads under two microjoules.

4
00:00:24.553 --> 00:00:30.512
Software teams usually treat databases, state machines, and memory caches as separate worlds

5
00:00:30.512 --> 00:00:36.601
that serialize text back and forth endlessly. Physically, storing a verified answer in aligned

6
00:00:36.601 --> 00:00:41.783
memory is simply freezing past computation so no machine ever pays for it twice.

7
00:00:42.283 --> 00:00:46.427
Across database dot openie dot dev and data dot openie dot dev, every

8
00:00:46.427 --> 00:00:51.711
state change clears a sixty-nine point seven microsecond safety gate before appending to

9
00:00:51.711 --> 00:00:57.537
a tamper-evident fingerprint-linked log indexed by both valid world time and recorded write time.

10
00:00:58.037 --> 00:01:02.779
Reading a verified entry from our zero-copy memory-mapped store took zero point three

11
00:01:02.779 --> 00:01:07.576
two five microseconds and one point seven seven microjoules on built-in chip counters,

12
00:01:07.576 --> 00:01:14.159
while deterministic logs replayed twenty-five thousand five hundred frames identically across three operating systems.

13
00:01:14.659 --> 00:01:19.968
Mapping ten storage and state coordinates onto real joules and microseconds clarifies

14
00:01:19.968 --> 00:01:24.215
exactly when to compute fresh and when to read directly from memory.
